            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "publicationNumber": "10127",
        "doi": "10.1049/ic:20040553",
        "publicationYear": "2004",
        "publicationDate": "3-3 Nov. 2004",
        "articleNumber": "1514285",
        "articleTitle": "Streaming AV around the home using 802.11",
        "volume": null,
        "issue": null,
        "startPage": "117",
        "endPage": "127",
        "publisher": "IET",
        "articleContentType": "Conferences",
        "publicationTitle": "The IEE Storage and Home Networks Seminar, 2004.",
        "authors": [
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "J. Prince",
                "firstName": "J.",
                "lastName": "Prince"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "IEEE 802.11 (Wi-Fi) have the range and throughput needed to distribute AV around the home today. Wireless local area network implements wireless data networking using Ethernet type protocols. Many stations may wish to share the medium to send frames. IEEE 802.11 uses CSMA/CS-ACK to arbitrate access. Continued development built on proven MAC technology delivers even higher throughputs.",
        "keywords": {
            "Index Terms": [
                "Multimedia",
                "Throughput",
                "Trademark",
                "GHz Band",
                "Co-channel Interference"
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "publicationNumber": "10127",
        "doi": "10.1049/ic:20040554",
        "publicationYear": "2004",
        "publicationDate": "3-3 Nov. 2004",
        "articleNumber": "1514287",
        "articleTitle": "Home networking - a manufacturers perspective",
        "volume": null,
        "issue": null,
        "startPage": "128",
        "endPage": "140",
        "publisher": "IET",
        "articleContentType": "Conferences",
        "publicationTitle": "The IEE Storage and Home Networks Seminar, 2004.",
        "authors": [
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "N. Shepherd",
                "firstName": "N.",
                "lastName": "Shepherd"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "Many of the issues that impeded the take-up of home networks in the past are now no longer valid. At the same time, people's perceptions and needs have evolved that content is available and people actively want and need to distribute it within the home. There are still some technical issues to solve, but these are being solved under the auspices of the digital living network alliance (DLNA) in conjunctions with expert groups like WiFi and UPnP. Consumer products are now available which build on the technical work and which fulfill people's needs.",
        "keywords": {}
    },
    {
        "publicationNumber": "10127",
        "doi": "10.1049/ic:20040555",
